</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition. Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection.</span> Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\{(u_{1},\mathbf{c}_{1}),(u_{2},\mathbf{c}_{2}),\dots,(u_{n},\mathbf{c}_{n})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\{(u_{1},\mathbf{c}_{1}),(u_{2},\mathbf{c}_{2}),\dots,(u_{n},\mathbf{c}_{n})\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> users, where each user <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> cultural background attributes (e.g., nationality, religion), denoted as <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{i}=\{c_{i1},c_{i2},\dots,c_{ik}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{i}=\{c_{i1},c_{i2},\dots,c_{ik}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a post <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal of culture-aware hate speech detection is to predict <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{hate}|u_{i},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>hate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{hate}|u_{i},p)</annotation></semantics></math> or equivalently <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{hate}|\mathbf{c}_{i},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>hate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{hate}|\mathbf{c}_{i},p)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the likelihood that user <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> would consider post <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as hateful, conditioned on the user’s cultural background <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by modeling cultural hate perception (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Culture-Post Interaction Matrix ‣ 3 Method ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), which is then used to construct individual hate perception (Section <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Individual Hate Perception Embedding ‣ 3 Method ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and is finally employed for classification (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Classification ‣ 3 Method ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Culture-Post Interaction Matrix</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better model individual hate perception and alleviate data sparsity, we shift our modeling target from individual to specific cultural background combinations. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the power set of user <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s cultural background <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})=\{\,s\mid s\subseteq\{c_{i1},c_{i2},\dots,c_{ik}\}\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})=\{\,s\mid s\subseteq\{c_{i1},c_{i2},\dots,c_{ik}\}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}(c_{i})|=2^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}(c_{i})|=2^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}=(\text{Male, US, Christian})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Male, US, Christian</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}=(\text{Male, US, Christian})</annotation></semantics></math> then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(c_{i})=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;\text{Male},\text{US},\text{Christian},\{\text{Male, US}\}\\
&amp;\{\text{US, Christian}\},\{\text{Male, Christian}\},\\
&amp;\{\text{Male, US, Christian}\}\end{aligned}\right\};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>Male</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>US</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Christian</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Male, US</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>US, Christian</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Male, Christian</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Male, US, Christian</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(c_{i})=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;\text{Male},\text{US},\text{Christian},\{\text{Male, US}\}\\
&amp;\{\text{US, Christian}\},\{\text{Male, Christian}\},\\
&amp;\{\text{Male, US, Christian}\}\end{aligned}\right\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to predict <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{hate}|g(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})),p_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>hate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{hate}|g(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})),p_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates the effects of all combinations to model an individual’s hate perception. Modeling combinations offers a practical benefit: even when an unseen user <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has incomplete or unseen background information, we can approximate their prediction by utilizing overlapping combinations observed in the dataset, i.e.</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{hate}|g(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{l})\cap(\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})),p_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>hate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{hate}|g(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{l})\cap(\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}_{i})),p_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further alleviate data sparsity and label ambiguity, we aggregate annotation signals at combination-level. Concretely, for each background combination <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comb_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comb_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and post <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> we collect</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}U_{l,j}=\{\,(u,\mathbf{c})\in\mathcal{U}\mid\;&amp;comb_{l}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}),\\
&amp;u\in Label(p_{j})\,\}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}U_{l,j}=\{\,(u,\mathbf{c})\in\mathcal{U}\mid\;&amp;comb_{l}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c}),\\
&amp;u\in Label(p_{j})\,\}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">i.e., the set of users who possess combination <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comb_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comb_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and labeled <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This aggregation implements a single-direction label propagation from observed (higher-order) combinations toward their constituent combinations: labels provided at a richer (upper) combination inform estimates for its subsets. The intuition is that while a single annotator’s label does not reveal which attribute cased the judgment, pooling labels across users who share a combination yields more robust estimates of combination-level tendencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To further distinguish combinations, we treat each combination as a “document”, and its contributing <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\mathbf{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\mathbf{c})</annotation></semantics></math> pairs as “words”. Under this view we build a culture-post interaction matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> total combinations and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> posts) using TF–IDF weighting derived from aggregated labels and co-occurrence information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Individual Hate Perception Embedding</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We factorize <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to derive latent features of combinations and posts. Specifically, we initialize an embedding matrix for combinations <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, for posts <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and bias terms <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>z</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. The predicted score <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{l,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{l,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{l,j}=\mu+b_{c_{l}}+b_{w_{j}}+q_{j}^{T}p_{l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{l,j}=\mu+b_{c_{l}}+b_{w_{j}}+q_{j}^{T}p_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the global mean, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{c_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{c_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{w_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{w_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> are biases, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are latent vectors of combinations and posts. To learn these embeddings, we minimize the following objective:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{l,j}(Y_{l,j}-\hat{Y}_{l,j})^{2}+\lambda(b^{2}_{c_{l}}+b^{2}_{w_{j}}+\lVert q_{j}\rVert^{2}+\lVert p_{l}\rVert^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l,j}(Y_{l,j}-\hat{Y}_{l,j})^{2}+\lambda(b^{2}_{c_{l}}+b^{2}_{w_{j}}+\lVert q_{j}\rVert^{2}+\lVert p_{l}\rVert^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls regularization and prevents overfitting by penalizing large parameter values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Individuals interpret hate speech from multiple perspectives, depending on which subset of their cultural attributes is active. Since embeddings of all combinations are aligned in the same latent space, we represent an individual’s hate perception embedding as a linear combination of all its combinations:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HP(u_{i})=\sum_{comb_{l}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c_{i}})}\alpha_{l}\begin{bmatrix}p_{l}\\
b_{c_{l}}\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HP(u_{i})=\sum_{comb_{l}\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{c_{i}})}\alpha_{l}\begin{bmatrix}p_{l}\\
b_{c_{l}}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable coefficient reflecting the relative influence of combination <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="comb_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">comb_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Classification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate the individual hate perception embedding with post features for classification. Given an individual <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a post <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the prediction is:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{hate}|u_{i},p_{j})=f_{\theta}(HP(u_{i}),q_{j},s_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>hate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{hate}|u_{i},p_{j})=f_{\theta}(HP(u_{i}),q_{j},s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the post’s interaction feature from Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Individual Hate Perception Embedding ‣ 3 Method ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the text embedding extracted by the CLIP text encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a classifier with parameters <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The model predicts that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> perceives <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as hateful if <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(hate\mid u_{i},p_{j})\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(hate\mid u_{i},p_{j})\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and non-hateful otherwise.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span>
we conduct experiments on CREHate dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, where each annotator has 8 different backgrounds. We randomly split the data at the post level into training/validation/test with a ratio of 70%/15%/15%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> To enable a comprehensive comparison, we evaluate against two groups of baselines: (1) Pretrained language models (PLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caselli et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbieri et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou (2020)</a></cite>: To adopt these models to our setting, we introduce additional learnable background tokens (e.g., “[male]” to indicate the user’s gender) and prepend them to the post text before fine-tuning, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2023a)</a></cite>. (2) Zero-Shot Prompting: We further test LLMs in zero-shot setting, including <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLama-2-7b-chat-hf</span> and GPT-5. The specific prompt templates and more details are described in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Prompts for Zero-shot Experiments ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Culturally-adapted PLMs ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Classification Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether models can effectively capture the relationship between text and cultural backgrounds. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Classification Evaluation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Seeing Hate Differently: Hate Subspace Modeling for Culture-Aware Hate Speech Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our proposed method outperform the best baseline by an average margin of 1.05% across all metrics. Since every model has access to the same set of posts during training, their differences in text encoding capability are minimal, which explains why PLMs achieve comparable results. This also highlights that PLMs share similar limitations in culturally-aware modeling, at least under the standard fine-tuning paradigm. Besides, although GPT-5 in the zero-shot setting yields relatively strong performance, it still lags behind fine-tuned models by a substantial margin.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Classification Performance</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:111.9pt;vertical-align:-53.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-15.6pt,5.1pt) scale(0.917147758543387,0.917147758543387) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Model</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Accuracy</td>
